lly want to watch  out or is honey which of course is a  risk and should not be giving it to  babies until they're at least one years  old out of fear of botulism as well as  added sugars and salt but pretty much  everything else is fair game  as long as there's some modification for  texture and size to make it a little bit  easier for baby to eat well you can  definitely progress over time to harder  firmer foods as baby homes their feeding  skills when you first get started you  want to offer fruits and vegetables that  are soft enough that you could kind of  mash them with your fingers or baby  could mash them with their gums or  tongue that way even if they don't have  teeth yet they could still mash up the  food enough that they could safely  swallow so some foods you would  definitely have to cook them like bake  them or steam them really well in order  to get them soft enough for baby  eat so things like baby carrots or sweet  potato would be a really great example  other foods are naturally really soft so  that you can just offer them as is like  avocado or bananas another great one so  when you're just getting started you  definitely want to avoid really hard raw  fruits and vegetables that could break  off into a chunk large enough that could  cause baby to choke so things like raw  carrots or raw Apple are not great  choices when you're just getting started  with baby lightly now when it comes to  shape most babies don't develop a pincer  grasp until about 9 to 10 months which  basically just means that they don't yet  know how to pick up foods with their  thumb and their finger so we want to  make it as easy as possible for baby to  kind of get food into their mouth I  don't know my son what specially was  just getting started basically would  just take his whole hand and kind of  just like smear it into his mouth so one  of the things I like to do is to cook  things into kind of long finger like  shapes whenever possible so baby carrots  are already like that they're great  broccoli I like to cook with a bit of a  stock at the end so it's an easy thing  for him to kind of hold up like a handle  and things like melon or other fruit you  can cut into strips on the other end of  the spectrum a lot of babies struggle  with really soft kind of slit  foods like avocado cuz they could just  kind of slip right out of their hand and  then of course you just lost that food  to the dog in my case usually so one of  the things I like to do is to roll those  really soft kind of sweat or juicier  foods like avocado or really really ripe  mango and some ground almonds or ground  flax or a baby cereal because not only  is that gonna help with the grip but it  also can add a lot of iron and healthy  fats and other nutrients it's also  important to note that some food shapes  are not safe for baby when you're just  getting started using baby led weaning  so things like really hard round foods  like little baby cherry tomatoes or  whole grapes can technically get lodged  in baby's throat and cause it to choke  so you can definitely still serve these  kinds of foods in fact the grapes and  tomatoes are babies literally they're  his favorite foods of all time but you  might just want to cut them in half or  in quarters depending on their size and  I get it I know every single time I say  the word choke you guys are thinking  this is terrifying I could kill my baby  I get it I was there and honestly every  single time baby eat gags little I hold  my breath but the reality is it is so  rewarding to see your kid being able to  eat all of these foods independently all  on his or her own not to mention choking  is far less common than we actually  think it is and this is a skill feeding  and feeding oneself is a skill that they  need to master and I'm really proud that  I feel like I've given my baby a really  great start at this all right so let's  move on to talking about grades so when  it comes to grains there are so many  great options and honestly my kid like  most kids is a Carbajal ik so this is  really fun for me but I love doing like  whole-wheat pasta or a bean based pasta  ideally in a shape that's a little  easier for baby e to grab so things like  rotini or fusilli or penne or rigatoni  all are fan favorites in this house  so for bread I typically like to cut  these into little strips so it's really  easy for baby e to pick up and sell feed  I also like to serve my bread as toast  with some kind of butter or nut butter  spread and the reason for this is that  fresh bread  to get kind of gummy in baby's mouth and  kind of gum up into a big bowl but  really dry bread without and kind of  spread or butter or anything kind of  yummy on it can be a choking hazard  because it is so dry so having some kind  of butter toast or nut butter toast is a  really nice balance  oatmeal or other porridge grains can  absolutely be served as we normally do  kind of like mush but using baby led  weaning principles what I recommend  doing is pre-loading a little bit on to  a spoon and then handing it over to baby  or letting them pick up the spoon from  the tray so that they can self feed  themselves rather than you spoon feeding  them yes I know it's so messy to let  them try to negotiate with a spoon but  so is everything else in Parenthood so  just get used to it  so other whole grains like quinoa or  buckwheat or wheat berries they're all  totally fair game you could just serve  them like grains as is however in my  experience they're so small it's very  difficult for kids to be able to pick  each of those things up and they'll  definitely get really frustrated not to  mention it'll be very very messy so  typically I like to kind of cook them  into pancakes or make them into little  fritters and I've got lots of recipes  for these kinds of things on my blog so  check that out in the description below  now let's move on to dairy just like  what we talked about with the oatmeal  you can absolutely preload a little bit  of full fat plain yogurt or ricotta  cheese onto a spoon and then let baby  feed him or herself also not  surprisingly he is my son baby eat is  obsessed with cheese  iczer cheese at least once a day because  he it's like such a treat for him so  typically what I like to do is cut it  into little baton like kind of small  finger so you can easily pick it up also  large shreds also work really well easy  for him to kind of grab little pieces  but I could probably stay away from  really hard cheeses like parmesan not to  mention it's a saltier cheese as well  and go for lower sodium options like  mozzarella I always have buffalo  mozzarella in my house because I love  caprese salad and it's definitely one of  his favorites too cuz it's naturally  lower in sodium and it's also super soft  so it's really easy to eat and it's  delish so why not so let's talk about  protein foods so I think that a lot of  parents find that getting those protein  who's into their kids is probably the  most challenging part when it comes to  feeding however it's arguably also the  most important iron is a really  important nutrient for new eaters  because by six months  breast milk no longer provides enough  iron for babies to meet their needs so  we want to make sure we're offering lots  of iron and protein rich foods like meat  poultry fish eggs and nuts all of which  are really great sources of iron to help  baby meet their needs when it comes to  meat moisture is key because really dry  meats and kind of get stuck in baby's  throat so I always look produce your  higher fat meats that can break down in  baby's mouth so things like meat balls  are gray or braised meats or fish and of  course you want to make sure that you're  carefully removing any little bones or  skin eggs you just want to make sure  that they're cooked through but you can  scramble them you can make it into an  omelet and cut it into strips or  frittata and cut it into strips so it's  really easy for baby to pick up and hold  and you can also feel free to add in any  of your favorite herbs spices and  veggies to expand baby's palate for  beans and legumes I love to preload a  little bit hummus onto meat or veggies  or toast and you can also add coke  legumes to your favorite pancake or  muffin or fritter recipe as for nuts I  would say avoid any cool raw nuts as  they could potentially be a choking  hazard but I think it's a great idea to  add a thin layer of peanut butter to  things like toast not too thick of  course because it can get stuck on the  roof of their mouth and also be a bit of  a choking hazard  but I also love adding nut butters to  any fritters or pancakes muffin batters  Oh meal literally anything that I'm  serving babyyyyy I try to sneak some nut  products in there at least every few  days and I'm definitely gonna do another  video on introducing allergens in the  near future but for now just know that  it is recommended that we introduce  these allergens early and a final note  on iron for your best absorption bang  for the buck you want to pair it with a  source of vitamin C so you'll see that I  always incorporate some kind of fruits  and vegetables into every iron rich meal  and then finally it let's talk fat well  not an official food group I do think  it's really important to talk about fat  because babies do not need a low-calorie  low-fat diet they need lots of healthy  fats in their diet to thrive and grow so  make sure that you're cooking with  generous amounts of  and incorporating lots of healthy fats  like avocado and nut butter so I hope  this little how-to gives you some  inspiration to get started with baby led  weaning if you like this video there's  gonna be so much more baby content  coming so be sure to subscribe to the  channel give this video the thumbs up  and leave me a comment below some of  your concerns or experiences around  starting solids hope to see you guys  soon  thanks so much bye

